A||N||9||Zorba||Water TEMP Sensor And V8 Set Up||||13||
Z||000000||Zorba||10-15-2000||01:30 PM||||I just realized that the PERFORMER RPM Manifold appears to have no provisions for the stock fiero temp sensor, other than two 1/2" openings for heater hoses outlets. The stock fiero water/gauge<BR>sensor does not fit into those holes. How have you solved this problem or how have you set up your fiero w/temp sensor for the stock gauge in the cabin? Thanks.||||reg||13
Z||000001||V8Fiero||10-15-2000||01:34 PM||||You can use a reducer bushing to adapt the sensor to the manifold. I had to do that on mine and it works fine. Not sure on the inside the cabin thingy.... hopes this helps. <BR>Scott||||reg||6
Z||000002||Zorba||10-15-2000||01:40 PM|||| Do you have the performer rpm manifold? It only has two openings for water outlets. Did you use the front or rear one for your fiero sensor? I am heading out to napa. They are not very smart here when it comes to "creative" fixing...they just want to know model year, car and the computer does the thinking, then they go to the back and pull stuff off the shelves...Hard to make them think!||||reg||1
Z||000003||V8Fiero||10-15-2000||03:25 PM||||No, I dont have the manifold, but I dont think it matters what one you use. I put mine closest to the water neck. My heater hose outlet was right next to it. I would just go to a hardware store and get the bushing you need. Thats what I did. Go to the plumbing section and use black pipe fittings.  Take your sensor with you to make sure you get the right one. Good Luck.\<P>P.S. I know what you mean about NAPA and all the other parts stores. Doesnt anyone that works at these stores ever heard of "custom".<P>Scott||||reg||4
Z||000004||Zorba||10-15-2000||05:32 PM|||| Hi...thanks for the hint. I foud the bushing and it fits fine. BUT...another question...if you use an electric water pump does one heater hose go to it and the other to the manifold? If that's the case, where does the hose that comes from the RIGHT SIDE water pipe go to ??? ||||reg||1
Z||000005||V8Fiero||10-15-2000||06:28 PM||||Hmmmm, Now Im stumped. Have no experience with an electric pump. Anyone else? Sorry cant help with that one.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000006||rprevo||10-15-2000||10:50 PM|||| If it's a Morrosso I ran mine like this,<BR>when the thermostat is closed I send the<BR>fluid out of the intake through the heater<BR>hose into the heater core out of the heater<BR>core into the hose that conects to the<BR>top of the water pump.<BR>This set-up does 2 things,<BR>1.It sends hot coolant through the heater<BR>core first, Almost instant heat in the car.<P>2. The line comming from the heater core<BR>going back to the pump acts as a self primer.<P> I had a hard time keeping my pump primed<BR>untill I tried this.<BR> I also mounted mine under the front<BR>hood in place of my spare tire and made<BR>a manifold for the front of the block.<BR>It works great, When I did my fathers<BR>car we put a mezier 35 GPM electric pump in.<BR>It is a far better pump. My car runs 210 degrees his hardly ever sees 190, Both engines around 330 Hp Chevys!<BR>Both pumps available through Summit,<P> Ray<BR> P.S.<BR> If your referring to the little line you<BR>can just plug it.<P><p>[This message has been edited by rprevo (edited 10-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Zorba||10-16-2000||12:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rprevo:<BR><B> <BR> I also mounted mine under the front<BR>hood in place of my spare tire and made<BR>a manifold for the front of the block.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Do you have a picture or more details on this set up? Considering the lack of space for the water pump in the engine bay, this sounds really interesting. I am particularly interested on how you hooked up the hoses to the water pump in relationship to the radiator, etc. Did you use any block off plates on the engine block with this set up?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||rprevo||10-16-2000||09:19 PM|||| What I did was ran the suction line of the radiator inline with rack and up into the spare tire space, The electric water pump is mounted to an aluminum block, this block serves as a manifold to feed the passenger side water pipe which is what supplies the block with cool water, At 19 GPM the most<BR>I ran in Temp was 225 at 70 MPH with the 4:10 gear in the 4 speed That was in the summer,<BR>As I said before with my Fathers we went with a Meizier Hi Volume 35 GPM 2000 Hr Waranty pump, His runs a constant 190 with a 190 thermostat, The pump is designed in California and sold through Summit and there Tech support was very Prompt and profesional,<BR>As far as pictures I have a picture of the pump in the tire area but not one from Underneath the car, I think I can E-Mail it but I'm not quite sure how to put it on the Forum,<BR> I'm very Computer Dumb!<P> But I got alot of compliments at cruises with the water pump set-up.<P> Ray<P><BR> ||||reg||6
Z||000009||pherder||10-16-2000||09:30 PM||||Ray,<P>I would be interested in a copy of your picture also!<P>BTW, what did you use to plumb the block?<P>I am going to use an Aerospace Components remote mount electric water pump.  It is rated @ 37 gal/min @ 4 amps.  Got this pump thru JEGS.<P>Paul||||reg||5
